1. Fix HotReloadWatcher so that a transition from "missing" to "present" triggers the reload callback (or treat first successful read after error as change). But modifying core watcher semantics affects other users — only StationCatalogReloader uses it though (plus unit tests).
2. Fix StationCatalogReloader::Tick: if `!loaded_`, attempt TryLoad. That is: when catalog not yet loaded, keep trying to load (count as reload when it succeeds). Simple and localized:

```cpp
bool StationCatalogReloader::Tick() {
  const auto before = reload_count_;
  watcher_.Tick();
  if (!loaded_) {
    TryLoad(true);  // 文件补齐后首次成功加载也算一次换新
  }
  ...
}
```

Hmm, but wait — TryLoad with count_as_reload=true increments reload_count_ only on success. If still failing, last_error_ is set. Good. But careful: watcher_.Tick() may set last_error_ too; ordering fine.

But is this in scope? The task requirements say modify launch file and map_ingest_node.cpp/.hpp. The review finding C1 says "This closes the loop so StationCatalogReloader watches the actual file emitted by map_ingest_node!" The parent may not be aware of the first-appearance gap. Should I fix core? The instruction says requirements: modify those files. But if I only repoint stations_file to the generated path and the dispatcher can't pick up the file on first appearance, the loop is not actually closed in the common case (dispatcher starts, then ingest writes file). That would make the C1 fix ineffective in practice.

Let me verify the exact behavior once more:

StationCatalogReloader constructor: TryLoad(false) — file missing → loaded_=false, last_error_ set. watcher_.Tick() — last_write_time fails → last_error_ = ec.message(), return. initialized_ stays false.

dispatcher reload_timer_ at 1s: Tick() → watcher_.Tick() → file still missing → error. `!loaded_` so with my proposed fix TryLoad fails again, reload_count_ unchanged → returns false, dispatcher logs throttled warn. Good.

When map_ingest writes the file: next Tick() → watcher_.Tick() → mtime OK → `!initialized_` → baseline set, return (no reload callback!). Then `!loaded_` → TryLoad(true) succeeds → loaded_=true, reload_count_++ → returns true → dispatcher logs "hot-reloaded" and syncs SkeletonPlanner. 

Without the core change: watcher baseline established, no reload, catalog never loads until second mtime change. Broken in the common case.

So I should make the core change too (minimal, in StationCatalogReloader::Tick). Also check the unit test for the reloader — test at line 102: `EXPECT_FALSE(reloader.Tick())` after making file invalid; with my change, loaded_ is already true so `!loaded_` is false, no behavior change. Test line 79: file loaded, mtime unchanged → Tick returns false; loaded_ true, fine. Test line 94: mtime changed → reload. Fine.

Are there tests where reloader starts with missing file? Let me check test_station_catalog.cpp around those lines to be sure.

Also visualizer: it loads stations once at construction; if the generated file doesn't exist yet, it uses defaults and never reloads. Should the visualizer also hot-reload? The review finding only says dispatcher_node and dispatcher_visualizer_node should use the generated stations_file path. The visualizer loading at construction with a missing file → catch exception → defaults. Hmm, that's a pre-existing behavior (visualizer has no hot reload at all, even with static stations_file it never reloads). Changing visualizer to hot-reload is beyond the review scope; but at minimum, if the file doesn't exist at visualizer startup, YAML::LoadFile throws, caught, uses hardcoded defaults — degraded but not crashing. 

Could I make visualizer retry loading? A light-touch improvement: in its 500ms timer, if stations_ came from fallback (or file missing), retry LoadStations. Actually the review only asked to fix the path wiring for the visualizer. Let me not gold-plate but consider: with use_map_server:=true, visualizer will typically start before generated file exists → shows fake default stations forever. That's a real degradation introduced by repointing the path (before, it pointed at the static share file which always exists). Hmm — actually before the C1 fix, visualizer pointed at static stations.yaml which exists in share (installed). So it worked. After repointing to generated path, in the common race (visualizer starts before ingest), it falls to defaults and stays wrong forever. That makes the C1 fix introduce a regression in visualization.

So a minimal retry in the visualizer makes the fix coherent: reload stations on the timer if not loaded from file yet, or simply reload when the file mtime changes... Simplest robust approach: track stations_loaded_ok_; in the 500ms timer, if !stations_loaded_ok_ or file mtime changed, try LoadStations again. Actually simplest: reload when mtime changes (hot reload like dispatcher). Let me look at the full visualizer file to decide a minimal change.

Also for the dispatcher: repointing stations_file to the generated path when use_map_server is true. How to express conditionally in launch? Use PythonExpression:

```python
from launch.substitutions import PythonExpression

stations_file_default = PythonExpression([
    "'", generated_stations_share, "' if '", use_map_server, "' == 'true' else '", stations_file_share, "'"
])
```

But careful: LaunchConfiguration use_map_server default 'false'. PythonExpression evaluates to a string; DeclareLaunchArgument default_value can be a substitution. Then add a `stations_file` launch argument with that default, and use LaunchConfiguration('stations_file') in dispatcher and visualizer parameters. That also gives operators an override slot. The review suggests either approach; adding a `stations_file` launch arg with conditional default is clean.

Edge: `use_map_server` could be 'True' or 'true'? CLI accepts 'true'/'false' typically (launch conditions parse case-insensitively, but PythonExpression compares literally). Use `.lower()`? In PythonExpression, we can generate: `"'{gen}' if str('{use}').lower() == 'true' else '{static}'"`. The substitution values get embedded as text into the expression; LaunchConfiguration expands to its string value. So expression text: `'.../generated/stations.yaml' if str('false').lower() == 'true' else '.../stations.yaml'`. Fine.

Path quoting: paths contain no quotes; the workspace path /home/ubuntu/个人学习/... contains Chinese chars but that's fine in Python string. But wait — single quotes in path would break; there are none.

Now the I1 fix — mix origin into ComputeMapHash:

```cpp
const auto mix_double = [&mix](double v) {
  uint64_t bits;
  std::memcpy(&bits, &v, sizeof(v));
  mix(&bits, sizeof(bits));
};
mix_double(grid.origin_x);
mix_double(grid.origin_y);
mix_double(grid.origin_yaw);
```

Place after resolution, before data. Endianness: FNV over raw bytes is fine for same-machine dedup; note hash is also written to catalog map_hash for version gate comparison — consistent across runs on same machine. Fine.

I2 fix — cache last_map_msg_, add retry timer:

Header changes:
- Add `void ProcessMap();` and store `OccupancyGrid::SharedPtr last_map_;` and `rclcpp::TimerBase::SharedPtr retry_timer_;`.

Restructure: MapCallback validates msg, stores it, calls ProcessMap(). ProcessMap() uses cached msg. Timer at e.g. 1Hz: if last_map_ && last_ingest_hash_.empty() → ProcessMap(). But also "or markers file becomes available" and "edited markers.yaml automatically gets ingested" — the review says: "If last_map_msg_ is available and last_ingest_hash_ is empty (or markers file becomes available), trigger ProcessMap()". Also "late-arriving or edited markers.yaml automatically gets ingested".If we only retry when last_ingest_hash_ empty, then edited markers.yaml after successful ingest won't re-ingest. The review text mentions "edited markers.yaml automatically gets ingested" — to support edits, we could track the markers file mtime and re-process on change. Simplest that satisfies: on each timer tick, if last_map_ exists, compute anchors digest... that's basically re-running LoadAnchors each second and comparing ingest hash — ProcessMap already handles dedup via last_ingest_hash_. So simplest: timer calls ProcessMap() whenever last_map_ is set; ProcessMap early-returns cheaply if hash unchanged. But LoadAnchors re-reads YAML every second — that's fine (small file), and matches "edited markers.yaml automatically gets ingested". But there's a wrinkle: if ingest failed (pipeline error), retrying every second forever spams errors. Use RCLCPP_ERROR_THROTTLE for failure logs in the retry path? Currently errors are logged each attempt. With a 1Hz timer, spam. Options: only auto-retry when last_ingest_hash_ empty (initial ingestion pending) — covers "markers file late/missing" and "map arrived before markers ready". For "edited markers.yaml" after successful ingest, mtime-driven detection would be needed... The review's fix requirement literally: "If last_map_msg_ is available and last_ingest_hash_ is empty (or markers file becomes available), trigger ProcessMap()". So gate on last_ingest_hash_.empty(). Edited markers after success: the operator can re-send /map or restart; or we can also track markers mtime. Let me add markers mtime tracking — it's cheap and satisfies "edited markers.yaml automatically gets ingested":

In ProcessMap (or timer): if last_map_ && (last_ingest_hash_.empty() || markers_mtime != last_markers_mtime_) → ProcessMap. Hmm, but ProcessMap reads anchors via LoadAnchors; we can capture mtime of markers_file inside timer callback before calling ProcessMap, store last seen mtime. Keep it simple:

```cpp
retry_timer_ = create_wall_timer(1s, [this]() {
  if (!last_map_) return;  // /map 还没来 if (last_ingest_hash_.empty()) {
    ProcessMap();  // 初始导入未完成（markers 文件晚到/写文件失败等）
    return;
  }
  // 初始导入成功后：markers 文件被编辑也要触发重导
  std::error_code ec;
  const auto mtime = std::filesystem::last_write_time(markers_file_, ec);
  if (!ec && mtime != last_markers_mtime_) {
    last_markers_mtime_ = mtime;
    ProcessMap();
  }
});
```

Wait — but on success, set last_markers_mtime_ = mtime inside ProcessMap? Better: in ProcessMap after successful ingest, record markers mtime (if file exists). Also note: ProcessMap when called from MapCallback — the /map callback shouldn't duplicate timer logic; MapCallback: validate, cache, ProcessMap(), and also record markers mtime on success (inside ProcessMap).

Edge: markers_file_ empty → LoadAnchors returns empty → ProcessMap logs error "No station anchors available" and returns. With timer gating, if markers_file_ empty, last_markers_mtime_ never set; last_ingest_hash_ stays empty → timer retries ProcessMap every second → error log spam every second. Use throttled error when called from timer... Simpler: in the timer callback, before calling ProcessMap, check: if markers_file_.empty() return; and in ProcessMap use RCLCPP_ERROR (from map callback) vs throttle? Let me make ProcessMap log with RCLCPP_ERROR_THROTTLE (10s) for the "no anchors" and failure cases — throttled logs are fine from the map callback too (only one message per burst anyway).

Actually also failure paths: BuildTopologyCatalog failure → error each attempt,1Hz spam. Throttle those too. Simplest: convert the error logs in ProcessMap's retry-able failure paths to RCLCPP_ERROR_THROTTLE with 10s period.

Also the "waiting" warns in LoadAnchors when file doesn't exist — called from timer at 1Hz → warn spam. Throttle those as well, or only warn from the map callback. Let me add a parameter... simpler: change LoadAnchors warns to RCLCPP_WARN_THROTTLE (10s). LoadAnchors is const; RCLCPP_WARN_THROTTLE needs get_logger() and *get_clock() — fine in const method (get_clock() is const).

Hmm wait, LoadAnchors is const and RCLCPP macros with throttle use get_clock() — rclcpp::Node::get_clock() is const. OK.

Also dedup check: currently anchors.empty() check comes BEFORE hash compute — good, keeps that.

One more subtlety: ProcessMap takes the cached message. MapCallback validates width/height/data size; keep validation in MapCallback before caching (invalid messages shouldn't overwrite a good cached map). ProcessMap assumes valid cached msg.

Also thread safety: single-threaded executor (rclcpp::spin with default single-threaded) → no mutex needed.

